WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.810
Film Courage: What did you do to get better
as a writer from 10 years ago all the way

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:05.810
to now?

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.569
Whether it’s been the 10,000 hours we talked
about?

00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:09.250
Christine Conradt, Screenwriter: A lot of
it is the 10,000 hours.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:14.820
I mean, I’m a big believer in that if you
put in your 10,000, you’re at a place where

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.410
you just have confidence and you know what
you’re doing and so the obstacles that you

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:25.780
encounter you just know how to tackle and
so that’s very different than I think that

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.910
I was 10 years ago before I had done that.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:34.170
But I think also just being able to recognize
your deficiencies.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:39.519
For me when I read a great script and I go
“Oh my gosh, this is a great script,”

00:00:39.519 --> 00:00:41.309
I get really excited about it.

00:00:41.309 --> 00:00:42.969
What are they doing that I’m not doing?

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:46.289
What can I learn from what this writer did?

00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:53.090
And I think just constantly learning and constantly
reading and constantly researching and having

00:00:53.090 --> 00:01:00.179
that in the back of your mind all of the time,
that you want to get better and you’ll just

00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:05.460
gravitate toward things that make you better
instead of finding that comfortable place

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.170
where you know you are good at writing this.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:09.940
So I’m just going to write this over and
over.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.300
I think forcing yourself to stretch.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.880
Because once you do that, again it’s a great
feeling when you’ve written something good

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.220
and you’ve done something you haven’t
done before then you have something you are

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.220
really proud of.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.490
Film Courage: I’m wondering what scripts
those are?

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.890
Do you have one off the top of your head that
you looked at and was like “Wow I wish I

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:28.890
had written that?”

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.020
Or “I want to do something like this?”

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.210
Christine Conradt: It happens all the time.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.420
I can’t think of a specific one that’s
just come out.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:43.860
But I was just speaking on a panel and we
were reading the first five pages of a bunch

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.640
of people’s scripts and then we were critiquing
them (the whole panel was) and we didn’t

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.500
know whose was whose.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.110
And there was one that I thought was great
that I really gravitated to.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.711
So this guy came up afterward and was like
“Would you like to read it?

00:01:54.711 --> 00:01:56.610
It was my script that you liked?”

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.700
And I read it and I just thought it was really
fantastic.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:04.460
He’s a great writer and I remember reading
it and it was an action script and I don’t

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:06.350
typically write action.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:13.319
It was so crisp and it was just moving so
quickly and I was like I just love how he’s

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:19.190
structured this and how even on the page (the
way it looks), just so conducive to what he

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:24.430
was trying to convey in terms of content that
I was like but I’ve got to remember.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:29.870
So as I was reading that I was absorbing all
that because he may not be as far on his journey

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:34.489
as I am, but he’s certainly a better action
writer than I am and so I was learning a lot

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:38.420
from how he structured…the words he chose.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.900
Film Courage: Yeah and that’s where you
were talking earlier and I’m not sure if

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.239
the word was bitterness but that’s where
you get that chance to either take it from

00:02:47.239 --> 00:02:49.510
[a place of] bitterness to so “Wow, what
can I learn?

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:51.299
How can I do something similar?”

00:02:51.299 --> 00:02:54.030
Christine Conradt: You can learn from everyone
and that’s the thing, there’s so many

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:59.040
people who I think…people just want to feel
validated so badly where they get to a place

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.230
where they are like “I’m the expert and
everyone should be listening to what I have

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.689
to say about this.”

00:03:03.689 --> 00:03:10.670
And it’s kind of crazy because yeah you
may have put your 10,000 hours in, you may

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.079
be very good at what you do.

00:03:12.079 --> 00:03:16.629
But you can learn something from everything
that you read even learning what not to do.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:20.709
But even in really bad scripts you’ll find
these little moments where I’m like “Oh

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:25.329
my gosh, that was really well conveyed.”

00:03:25.329 --> 00:03:30.870
So just being open to those things and learning
from other people because people are constantly

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:36.290
adding to this body of work that exists in
the world and there is so much for you to

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.049
be able to read out there.

00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:44.079
Be open to what other people are doing, but
then again you have to love reading.

00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:46.569
So many writers don’t love reading.

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:50.260
So read, read, read, read and see what other
people are doing.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.639
A lot of people start writing without having
read a script and I just don’t understand

00:03:54.639 --> 00:03:55.639
it.

00:03:55.639 --> 00:03:56.809
Film Courage: Are you also reading novels,
too?

00:03:56.809 --> 00:03:58.669
Christine Conradt: When I have time.

00:03:58.669 --> 00:04:00.760
I don’t have a lot of time.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:04.060
But yeah, when I get the chance, yeah I love
to read novels.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:07.359
Film Courage: Yeah, it’s such a lost art
and I miss it, too.

00:04:07.359 --> 00:04:12.419
And it’s really hard in today’s world
because so much amazing content in on Youtube,

00:04:12.419 --> 00:04:15.129
so we can learn something even faster by watching
a video.

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:18.590
But I miss the act of reading and it seems
lost.

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:24.010
Christine Conradt: And I even get caught up
sometimes in reading nonfiction because I

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:26.620
feel like well at least I’m learning more
than if I’m reading a novel.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:30.070
But you should remember you are learning.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:35.300
By getting caught up in it and seeing how
that writer has taken you through this flow

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:40.039
of their words you’re learning a lot by
just doing that, too that you can then bring

00:04:40.039 --> 00:04:41.199
to screenwriting as well.

